2. How do you handle Distributed Transactions?
Problem:
Multiple microservices updating different databases.

Example
Order Service 
Payment Service
 Inventory Service
 If Payment succeeds and Inventory fails, data becomes inconsistent.
 Solution 1: Saga Pattern (Recommended)
Most commonly used.

What is Saga Design Pattern?

Saga Pattern is a microservices design pattern used to manage distributed transactions across multiple services.

Instead of having one big database transaction, Saga breaks the business transaction into a series of local transactions.

Each service:

Executes its own transaction.
Updates its own database.
Triggers the next service.

If any step fails, Saga executes compensating transactions to undo the completed steps and maintain consistency.

Why do we need Saga?
Problem without Saga

Suppose an e-commerce application has:

Order Service
Payment Service
Inventory Service
Shipping Service
Flow:
Create Order
      ↓
Payment Success
      ↓
Inventory Reserved
      ↓
Shipping Created

What happens if:
Order Created ✅
2
Payment Success ✅
3
Inventory Reserved ✅
4
Shipping Failed ❌
Now the system is inconsistent:

Customer was charged
Inventory was reduced
Product was not shipped

Traditional database rollback is not possible because each microservice has its own database.

Saga solves this problem.
How Saga Works:
Success Scenario:
Order Service
      ↓
Payment Service
      ↓
Inventory Service
      ↓
Shipping Service
      ↓
Order Completed

Failure Scenario:
If Shipping fails:
Shipping Failed
      ↓
Inventory Compensation
      ↓
Release Stock
      ↓
Payment Compensation
      ↓
Refund Customer
      ↓
Order Compensation
      ↓
Cancel Order

This rollback logic is called a Compensating Transaction

Types of Saga:
1. Choreography (Event-Driven)
Services communicate through events.
Order Created Event
        ↓
Payment Service
        ↓
Payment Completed Event
        ↓
Inventory Service
        ↓
Inventory Reserved Event
        ↓
Shipping Service
Tools:

Kafka
RabbitMQ
Event Bus

Advantages

Loose coupling
No central coordinator

Disadvantages

Difficult to track flow
Complex when many services exist

2. Orchestration (Central Coordinator)

Saga Orchestration (Central Coordinator) - Easy Explanation
In Orchestration Saga, a separate service called Saga Orchestrator controls the entire workflow.

Instead of services talking to each other directly, all services communicate through the orchestrator.

Example: E-Commerce Order

Services:

Order Service
Payment Service
Inventory Service
Shipping Service
Saga Orchestrator

Successful Flow
Customer Places Order
