Saga Design Pattern is used to handle distributed transactions in microservices architectures. Instead of using a single ACID transaction across multiple services, Saga breaks a business transaction into multiple local transactions. Each service updates its own database and triggers the next service. If any step fails, compensating transactions are executed to undo previously completed operations and maintain data consistency. Saga can be implemented using Choreography (event-driven with Kafka/RabbitMQ) or Orchestration (central coordinator). It is commonly used in e-commerce, payment, booking, and order management systems.

In Saga Orchestration, a central Saga Orchestrator controls the execution of distributed transactions across multiple microservices. The orchestrator invokes each service in sequence and decides whether to continue or execute compensating transactions when a failure occurs. It provides better visibility and control than Choreography and is commonly implemented using workflow engines such as Temporal, Camunda, Conductor, or custom Spring Boot orchestrator services.

Key Interview Point:

Choreography → Services communicate through Kafka/RabbitMQ events.
Orchestration → One central service controls the complete workflow. ✅

Most enterprise Spring Boot projects prefer Orchestration when the business flow is complex and requires centralized tracking.
